# dyn.md D-49 (and §15 Q4, ruled): the x86-64 IBT PLT (`.plt` lazy
# entries with `endbr64` and `.plt.sec`) stays refused at M2. Besides
# `-z ibt`/`-z ibtplt` (refused options of the table), it is chosen when
# every input relocatable object is marked IBT in `FEATURE_1_AND`
# (x64.md X-41, X-43). Debian's crt objects carry no property, so a
# default line never selects it; here every object is IBT|SHSTK
# (`-fcf-protection=full`, and the program's own `_start` instead of the
# crt files) and calls `puts` and `exit` through the PLT.
#
# What the references do (measured): GNU ld and lld write `.plt` +
# `.plt.sec` and mark the output IBT|SHSTK; mold marks it and writes its
# own IBT `.plt`; all three run. mortar refuses by name (WP-055).
#@ spec: dyn.md "D-49"
#@ arch: x86_64

cc -O2 -fPIE -fcf-protection=full start.c -o start.o
cc -O2 -fPIE -fcf-protection=full main.c -o main.o
link(bfd,lld,mold) gcc -pie -nostartfiles start.o main.o -o prog
run(bfd,lld,mold) prog
  stdout: hello ibt
check(bfd,lld,mold) readelf -nW prog
  has: x86 feature: IBT, SHSTK
link(mortar) gcc -pie -nostartfiles start.o main.o -o prog
  exit: fail
  diag(mortar): unsupported feature: IBT PLT (-z ibt, -z ibtplt, or every input marked IBT) in a dynamic executable

#--- start.c
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void);

void _start(void) { exit(main()); }
#--- main.c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
  puts("hello ibt");
  return 0;
}
